              },
              "code": "ORA_LLM_MODEL_PROVIDER_CERT_BASIC_LLAMA_3_3_70B",
              "model": "ORA_LLM_MODEL_GPT_OSS_120B",
              "modelName": "Gpt Oss",
              "provider": "CERT_BASIC"
            },
            "errorNodeId": "1773637992507",
            "name": "Build Requirement Contexts"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1774342664014"
          },
          "code": "PRJ_PJF_LLM_BUILD_REQUIREMENT_CONTEXTS",
          "id": "1774342581414",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1774342581416_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Generate four self-contained context strings for downstream workflow stages.\n\nUse this node for new requests, approvals, and all active inner-lifecycle user turns between request initiation and approval. Valid stages/routes include `NEW_REQUEST`, `APPROVED`, `REVISION_REQUESTED`, `INTERPRET_MISSING_DETAILS_REPLY`, `INTERPRET_TEMPLATE_REPLY`, `WAIT_USER_DECISION`, `PLAN_REQUEST_SCOPE`, `RESOLVE_PROJECT`, `RESOLVE_SOURCE_TASKS_RESOURCES`, `RESOLVE_TEMPLATE`, `RESOLVE_TASKS_RESOURCES`, `VALIDATE_REVIEW_READY`, and `VALIDATE_APPROVAL_READY`.\n\n## Inputs\n- Active Atomic Agent Details: `{{$context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvActiveAtomicAgentDetails}}`\n- Strategic Attach Content: `{{$context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvStrategicAttachContent}}`\n- Latest User Input: `{{$context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvLatestUserMessage}}`\n- Current Conversation Stage: `{{(($context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvWorkflowStage=='COMPLETED' || $context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvWorkflowStage=='CANCELLED' || $context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvWorkflowStage=='FAILED') && ($context.$nodes.PRJ_PJF_LLM_CLASSIFY_LATEST_USER_INTENT.$output.operationKind=='ACTIONABLE' && $context.$nodes.PRJ_PJF_LLM_CLASSIFY_LATEST_USER_INTENT.$output.confidence!='LOW')) ? 'NEW_REQUEST' : $context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvWorkflowStage}}`\n- Terminal Actionable Restart: `{{(($context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvWorkflowStage=='COMPLETED' || $context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvWorkflowStage=='CANCELLED' || $context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvWorkflowStage=='FAILED') && ($context.$nodes.PRJ_PJF_LLM_CLASSIFY_LATEST_USER_INTENT.$output.operationKind=='ACTIONABLE' && $context.$nodes.PRJ_PJF_LLM_CLASSIFY_LATEST_USER_INTENT.$output.confidence!='LOW')) ? 'true' : 'false'}}`\n- Chat History: `{{(($context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvWorkflowStage=='COMPLETED' || $context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvWorkflowStage=='CANCELLED' || $context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvWorkflowStage=='FAILED') && ($context.$nodes.PRJ_PJF_LLM_CLASSIFY_LATEST_USER_INTENT.$output.operationKind=='ACTIONABLE' && $context.$nodes.PRJ_PJF_LLM_CLASSIFY_LATEST_USER_INTENT.$output.confidence!='LOW')) ? '' : (($context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvStrategicAgentChatHistory)?$context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvStrategicAgentChatHistory:$context.$system.$chatHistory)}}`\n- Current Project Details: `{{$context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvExtractedProjectAttributes}}`\n- Current Final Tasks: `{{$context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvFinalProjectTasks}}`\n- Current Milestones: `{{$context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvExtractedProjectMilestones}}`\n- Current Task Resource Assignments: `{{$context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvTaskResourceAssignments}}`\n\n## Terminal Restart Contract\nIf Terminal Actionable Restart is `true`, the latest user input starts a new project initiation request after a closed or failed prior request. In that case, ignore Chat History, Current Conversation Stage, Current Project Details, Current Final Tasks, Current Milestones, Current Task Resource Assignments, and any prior cancellation, completion, or failure. Build all four contexts from Latest User Input only, expanded only to resolve references inside that latest input. Do not say no project creation is required because a prior request was cancelled, completed, or failed. Do not preserve prior project, template, task, milestone, resource, validation, creation, or response state. If Latest User Input contains `BU`, `Business Unit`, or `business unit`, the `projectRequirementContext` must preserve that as `Business Unit: <exact value>` instead of paraphrasing it as general ownership or audience. Preserve relative date words such as `today` together with the resolved date context when available.\n\n## Global Contract\n1. In the active inner lifecycle, any user message can revise any supported object. Do not limit interpretation to the object implied by the current route.\n2. Treat Latest User Input as a possible multi-object patch across project attributes, template intent, task plan, milestone plan, resources/task assignments, proposal intent, budget or financial-plan intent, review intent, approval, cancellation, or clarification.\n3. Stage/route is only context for disambiguation. It must not prevent project, template, task, milestone, resource, proposal, or budget changes from being represented in the relevant context strings.\n4. Preserve current known state unless Latest User Input explicitly changes, contradicts, removes, or replaces it.\n5. If the latest turn changes only one object, keep the other object contexts aligned to the current active request. Do not erase project details because the user selected a template. Do not erase tasks or milestones because the user changed Business Unit. Do not erase template intent because the user changed a date.\n6. Resolve references like \"change it\", \"same as above\", \"use this template\", \"keep the rest\", \"remove that task\", \"remove that milestone\", or \"looks good\" using stage, chat history, Current Project Details, Current Final Tasks, Current Milestones, and Current Task Resource Assignments.\n7. Output effective-state descriptions, not transcript summaries. Do not mention internal route names, stages, history, classification labels, or this prompt.\n8. Use explicit latest user values over previous state when they conflict.\n9. For `APPROVED`, reconstruct the approved effective request from the most recent review and active state. Do not return only approval words.\n10. For `WAIT_USER_DECISION`, distinguish approval, rejection, cancellation, questions, and revisions. If the user provides a revision, merge it into the relevant effective contexts.\n11. For `INTERPRET_TEMPLATE_REPLY`, always check whether the user also changed project attributes, tasks, milestones, resources, proposal intent, or budget intent.\n12. For `INTERPRET_MISSING_DETAILS_REPLY`, merge supplied missing values into the active request while preserving known values.\n\n## Downstream Query Contract\nEach output must be written as a fresh, actionable, object-focused query for the downstream node that consumes it. Do not write passive status summaries such as \"awaiting task details\" or \"no template has been selected\" by themselves.\n\nIf a user did not explicitly provide an object, still include the request scope and useful constraints that help downstream nodes select templates, extract source evidence, generate fallback task plans, or prepare resources. Do not ask the task extraction node to create a task plan when no explicit task or milestone evidence exists.\n- task context must describe the latest task and milestone requirement plus explicit task or milestone evidence from the latest user input, source document, or preserved current state. If no explicit task or milestone evidence exists, say that no explicit task or milestone list was provided while preserving the project scope, selected or requested template, dates, Business Unit, and task-relevant constraints for downstream template-task fetch or fallback generation. Do not instruct any downstream node to return empty arrays.\n- template context should tell template matching what kind of template to find from the project scope, industry/type, delivery pattern, dates, Business Unit, and commercial signals.\n- resource context inside the broad requirement context should retain implementation scope that helps resource extraction or assignment.\n\nUse phrases like \"No explicit task list or milestone list was provided by the user or source document\" when that is true. Do not imply the workflow is waiting for the user to provide tasks; downstream template-task fetch or fallback generation can handle planning after strict extraction returns empty.\n\n## Contexts To Produce\n\n### 1) `requirementContext`\nGeneral-purpose business request for downstream flows that still need broad context.\nRules:\n1. Return the final actionable business request, not commentary.\n2. Make it descriptive and self-contained.\n3. Include details the user has provided, confirmed, approved, or revised.\n4. Include project scope, requested objects, proposal/budget/resource intent, template preference, explicitly provided task or milestone intent, and review instructions when they affect the requested outcome.\n5. For `NEW_REQUEST`, return the user input, expanded only to resolve references.\n6. For active inner-lifecycle stages, merge Latest User Input into the current effective request and preserve unchanged project, template, task, milestone, resource, proposal, and budget intent.\n7. For `APPROVED`, reconstruct the fully approved request from the most recent review and active request state.\n\n### 2) `projectRequirementContext`\nProject-focused request for project attribute extraction.\nRules:\n1. Include project-level information only: scope, purpose, customer, project name, project number, start date, end date or duration, Business Unit, billing/commercial treatment, capitalizable treatment, burdening treatment, and project creation constraints when known.\n2. Do not include task breakdowns, milestone breakdowns, resource chatter, template discussion, proposal routing, or stage commentary unless they directly change the project-level request.\n3. For active inner-lifecycle stages, start from Current Project Details and merge latest project-level values or corrections. Preserve existing project fields unless Latest User Input explicitly changes, removes, or contradicts them.\n4. If Latest User Input is template-only, task-only, milestone-only, proposal-only, budget-only, approval-only, or a question, keep this context aligned to Current Project Details and the active project request. Do not omit known required values such as Business Unit or Project Start Date.\n5. If Latest User Input changes Business Unit during template selection, include the corrected Business Unit here so project extraction can update canonical project attributes before template matching repeats.\n6. If Latest User Input changes project name, number, customer, dates, duration, or commercial flags during review, include the revised value and preserve unchanged values.\n\n### 3) `taskRequirementContext`\nTask-and-milestone source-evidence request for strict extraction.\nRules:\n1. Focus only on tasks or milestones explicitly present in Latest User Input, source document text, Current Final Tasks, or Current Milestones.\n2. Explicit tasks include enumerated task lists, WBS rows, SOW deliverables, contract obligations, schedule rows, phases, workstreams, named activities, task additions/removals/replacements, or preserved current tasks. A broad project description such as \"software project\", \"code migration related task\", or \"implementation project\" is project scope, not a task list.\n3. Explicit milestones include named checkpoints, approvals, delivery events, phase gates, completion markers, payment triggers, acceptance events, go-live, award, kickoff, transition points, due dates in a milestone/deliverable schedule, milestone additions/removals/replacements, or preserved current milestones.\n4. For active inner-lifecycle stages, preserve Current Final Tasks and Current Milestones unless Latest User Input explicitly changes, contradicts, removes, or replaces them.\n5. If the user says to replace all tasks, describe only the replacement task plan and do not preserve old task rows except where the user asks to keep them.\n6. If the user says to replace all milestones, describe only the replacement milestone plan and do not preserve old milestones except where the user asks to keep them.\n7. If the user adds or removes only selected tasks or milestones, describe the resulting task and milestone plan by preserving unchanged items and applying the requested change.\n8. If no explicit task or milestone evidence exists, state that no explicit task list or milestone list is present in the user request, source document, or current preserved state, then include the latest project scope, selected or requested template, dates, Business Unit, and task-relevant constraints that may help template-task fetch or fallback generation. Do not say there is no explicit evidence when the document contains named SOW deliverables, contract obligations, schedule rows, phases, workstreams, named activities, phase gates, approvals, payment triggers, or delivery events. Do not instruct downstream nodes to return empty arrays.\n9. Do not create or invent a task plan in this context. Describe requirements and evidence only, and preserve broad task-relevant project scope for the nodes that perform template-task fetch or fallback generation.\n10. If milestones already exist and the latest turn does not explicitly revise milestones, say that the current milestones should be preserved unchanged while other requested revisions are applied.\n\n### 4) `templateRequirementContext`\nTemplate-focused request for template recommendation, selection, or normalization.\nRules:\n1. Focus on industry, project type, customer context, scope shape, delivery pattern, explicit source task/milestone structure when present, financial/commercial handling, Business Unit compatibility, and explicit template guidance or revision.\n2. Include project/task context only when it helps identify the right template or validate compatibility.\n3. For active inner-lifecycle stages, preserve active template intent and merge latest template preference, explicit template name, industry/type guidance, structure preference, default-template instruction, or compatibility correction.\n4. If Latest User Input is project-only, task-only, milestone-only, proposal-only, budget-only, approval-only, or a question, keep this context aligned to the current active request and do not erase selected/requested template intent.\n5. If the user supplies a direct template name after no templates were found, include that direct template name prominently and preserve corrected Business Unit or project type affecting compatibility.\n6. If the user changes Business Unit during template selection, include the corrected Business Unit as a compatibility signal and do not treat stale prior Business Unit as authoritative.\n7. If no explicit template was selected, write an actionable template-selection query using project scope, project type, industry/domain, delivery pattern, Business Unit, dates, commercial signals, and explicit source task/milestone structure only when present. State that no explicit template was selected only as a qualifier; do not return a template context that contains only absence of selection.\n8. Prefer wording like: \"Select or recommend a compatible project template for...\" followed by the project scope and compatibility constraints.\n\nReturn JSON only:\n{\n  \"requirementContext\": \"string\",\n  \"projectRequirementContext\": \"string\",\n  \"taskRequirementContext\": \"string\",\n  \"templateRequirementContext\": \"string\"\n}\n\nReturn only the final JSON object."
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n    \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n        \"requirementContext\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"projectRequirementContext\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"taskRequirementContext\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"templateRequirementContext\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        }\n    }\n}",
          "type": "LLM",
          "diagram": {
            "x": 10040.3125,
            "y": 7426
          }
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "modelConfiguration": {
