                  },
                  "code": "PRJ_PJF_LLM_EXTRACT_PROJECT_RESOURCES",
                  "id": "1773384101125",
                  "inputs": [
                    {
                      "id": "1773384101128_1",
                      "name": "prompt",
                      "type": "string",
                      "value": "- BEGIN LLM PROMPT -\nAnalyze the following plain text document describing a project. Determine whether it includes any Project Resources (defined below) and return ONLY a single JSON object in the exact structure specified.\n\nGoal: Identify resources used to execute/implement the project (the execution-side), not the client/owner/oversight side.\n\nDefinition: A Project Resource is a resource that contributes to accomplishing the overall objectives of a project. Resources are assigned to tasks to enable planning, assignment, scheduling, tracking, and reporting of progress and costs. They may be referred to as roles, jobs, people, nonlabor resources, or inventory items in the document.\n\nStep A - Party role detection (internal reasoning only; do not output this section):\n- From the document, infer and label each mentioned party as one of:\n  - Executor (implementer/delivery team performing design/build/test/operate/commission)\n  - Client (owner/employer/sponsor/recipient of deliverables/approver)\n  - Regulator/Authority (agencies, auditors, inspectors)\n  - Reviewer/Oversight (steering committees, owner's reps, client PMs)\n  - Other/Unknown\n- Heuristics for Executor (positive cues): \"contractor\", \"prime contractor\", \"subcontractor\", \"supplier installing\", \"vendor implementing\", \"integrator\", \"delivery team\", \"engineering firm\", \"EPC\", \"builder\", \"constructor\", \"implementation partner\", \"testing/commissioning team\", \"construction subcontractor\" when performing scope. Verbs like \"shall provide/perform/design/develop/engineer/build/install/test/startup/commission/maintain/deliver/produce/prepare/submit (as producer)\".\n- Heuristics for Client (negative cues): \"client\", \"owner\", \"employer\", \"sponsor\", \"buyer\", \"purchaser\", \"project authority\", \"BTR/Contract Specialist/Owner's Rep\", \"receives/approves/comments/accepts/oversees\", \"operations (as recipient)\".\n- Heuristics for Regulator/Authority: agencies and authorities (e.g., DOE, WDOH, AHJ), standards bodies.\n- If ambiguous whether a party is Executor vs Client, default to NOT including their resources.\n\nAllowed values\n- ResourceType (enum):\n  - Named Person\n  - Event Type\n  - Expenditure Category\n  - Expenditure Type\n  - Item Category\n  - Inventory Item\n  - Job\n  - Organization\n  - System Person Type\n  - Project Nonlabor Resource\n  - Resource Class\n  - Revenue Category\n  - Supplier\n  - Project Role\n- ResourceClassification (enum):\n  - Labor\n  - Equipment\n  - Material items\n  - Financial resources\n\nStrict inclusion/exclusion rules:\n- Include ONLY resources that clearly belong to the Executor side (the party/parties performing the implementation work). This includes the prime contractor and any lower-tier subcontractors/vendors when they are part of the implementation team performing scope.\n- EXCLUDE resources belonging to: Client/owner, regulators/authorities, reviewers/oversight, or purely administrative recipients.\n- Strong positive inclusion cues: resources tied to obligations like \"shall provide/perform/design/build/install/test/commission\", resources allocated to tasks/deliverables, design disciplines (civil/structural/mechanical/electrical/fire protection/HVAC), architects, geotechnical, land survey, PM, TL, schedulers, cost estimators, QA/QC, document authors producing deliverables, equipment and facilities used by the Executor (e.g., cranes, labs, servers, tools), inventory items consumed.\n- If a role appears for both Client and Executor (e.g., \"Project Manager\"), include only the Executor's instance.\n\nClassification rules (choose the single best type and classification using this priority and mapping):\n1) Named Person -> ResourceType = Named Person; ResourceClassification = Labor.\n2) Project Role (e.g., Project Manager, Architect, DBA, Functional Lead, Team Member, Task Lead) -> ResourceType = Project Role; ResourceClassification = Labor.\n3) Job (formal job title/code) -> ResourceType = Job; ResourceClassification = Labor.\n4) Organization (Executor-side divisions/cost centers allocated as resources) -> ResourceType = Organization; ResourceClassification = Labor.\n5) System Person Type (employee vs contingent worker at classification level) -> ResourceType = System Person Type; ResourceClassification = Labor.\n6) Project Nonlabor Resource (equipment/facilities/tools/time capacity consumed) -> ResourceType = Project Nonlabor Resource; ResourceClassification = Equipment.\n7) Inventory Item / Item Category (catalog/material items tracked for consumption/costs) -> ResourceType = Inventory Item or Item Category; ResourceClassification = Material items.\n8) Event Type / Expenditure Type / Expenditure Category / Revenue Category: Include ONLY if the document explicitly frames them as resources planned/assigned/consumed for project work or cost tracking (not merely metadata). If included -> ResourceClassification = Financial resources.\n9) Supplier: Include ONLY when the supplier is part of the Executor team performing implementation scope (e.g., install/commission). Map ResourceClassification by usage: hours/capacity -> Labor; equipment/facilities -> Equipment; goods/materials -> Material items; monetary-only allocation -> Financial resources. Otherwise exclude.\n10) Resource Class: If the document explicitly names one of the classes (Labor, Equipment, Material items, Financial resources) as the resource, set ResourceType = Resource Class and ResourceClassification to that class; else null.\n\nDates and deduping:\n- Dates: Normalize to YYYY-MM-DD when explicitly provided; otherwise null. Do not infer.\n- Deduplicate by ResourceName (case-insensitive). If multiple date ranges are found for the same resource, prefer earliest start and latest end; if conflicting or unclear, keep nulls.\n\nOutput must be valid JSON only. No comments, no trailing commas, no extra text.\n\nOutput JSON schema (structure and keys must match exactly):\n{\n  \"resourcesExist\": true or false,\n  \"resources\": [\n    {\n      \"ResourceName\": \"string\",\n      \"ResourceType\": \"Named Person | Event Type | Expenditure Category | Expenditure Type | Item Category | Inventory Item | Job | Organization | System Person Type | Project Nonlabor Resource | Resource Class | Revenue Category | Supplier | Project Role | null\",\n      \"ResourceClassification\": \"Labor | Equipment | Material items | Financial resources | null\",\n      \"ResourceStartDate\": \"YYYY-MM-DD or null\",\n      \"ResourceEndDate\": \"YYYY-MM-DD or null\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n- Document to analyze -\n---\nBEGIN PROJECT DOCUMENT  \n {{$context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvUploadedDocumentText}}\nEND PROJECT DOCUMENT\nBEGIN USER QUERY\n{{$context.$variables.prjPjfVarConvEffectiveRequirementContext}}\nEND USER QUERY\n---\n- END LLM PROMPT -"
                    }
                  ],
                  "outputSpecification": "{\"$schema\":\"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"resourcesExist\":{\"type\":\"boolean\"},\"resources\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"ResourceName\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"ResourceType\":{\"type\":[\"string\",\"null\"]},\"ResourceClassification\":{\"type\":[\"string\",\"null\"]},\"ResourceStartDate\":{\"type\":[\"string\",\"null\"]},\"ResourceEndDate\":{\"type\":[\"string\",\"null\"]}},\"required\":[\"ResourceName\",\"ResourceType\",\"ResourceClassification\",\"ResourceStartDate\",\"ResourceEndDate\"]}}}}",
                  "type": "LLM"
                },
